The yamlover editor - the state machine
description: the source projection's typing grammar, one state per subchapter
- >
  Each subchapter of this chapter is a **state** of the unlocked yamlover (source) editor - the
  cell tree drawn by `tools/yed`. A state page carries what is on screen and where the caret sits,
  and a **transitions table**: the event (a key press, a click) and the state it leads to, as a
  link. Conditional transitions list one row per condition. The executable mirror is
  `interpret(key, site)` in `tools/yed/src/grammar/dispatch.ts` - ONE pure table, run as data by
  `tools/yed/test/yed-dispatch.test.ts`; **the code table is normative**, and a grammar change
  edits the code, its test, and this chapter together ([the projectional
  editor](::server:editor)).
- >
  **Conventions.** `alphanumeric` covers letters, digits and any ordinary character that starts or
  extends a plain token - QUOTES INCLUDED: yed projects no quote glyphs and has no quote mode; a
  `"` is a character of the token, and an authored-quoted token commits with its spelling kept
  (raw-first). The keyed trigger mirrors how one types yamlover SOURCE - `key_colon_space` (a
  word, the colon, a space) puts the value INLINE on the same row, while `key_colon_enter` (the
  colon confirmed by Enter) puts the value on the NEXT rows. In VALUE position the same triggers
  open a nested structure COMPACTLY (`- name: `, `- - ` and their kin keep editing on the SAME
  row); only Enter breaks to the next row. A `key_colon` whose key ALREADY exists in the node is
  rejected with the error ring - keys are unique per node. Backspace in an empty value cell UNDOES
  the last structural token (the colon or the dash), never the whole entry. Clicking any cell
  enters that cell's focused state. A committed KEY is itself an editable cell - editing its text
  RENAMES the key on commit (the server routes the rename by STORAGE), and a duplicate key is
  rejected with the ring. Commits never lose focus - every transition that persists a value ALSO
  names the cell the caret lands in.
- >
  **THE PROJECTION RULE.** One machine, several PROJECTIONS. The pure yed state
  (`tools/yed/src/state.ts`: the parser IR document + the cursor) is what edits; a projection only
  DRAWS. The source projection draws rows of yamlover token cells and applies the typing grammar
  of this chapter. The [chapter projection](::server:chapter-editor) draws its own cell zoo over
  the same IR and does NOT use the typing grammar at all - no holes, no `- `/`key:` decisions.
- >
  **THE FORMAT RULE.** A block's format is its `!!<...$defs:X>` tag - table, bullets, numbered -
  and NORMAL CHAPTER is the untagged case, so choosing it DROPS the tag rather than stamping one.
  An untagged container INHERITS from its enclosing format, compounding down the whole chain: a
  sublist stays that list's kind at any depth, a table is exactly two levels (rows, then cells)
  and anything below a cell is a chapter again. The format is READ from the spine on demand, never
  stored.
- >
  **THE LEVEL RULE.** Enter after committing a value DESCENDS - the entry hole it opens lives
  INSIDE the just-committed node: the value becomes the node's own scalar line and the node is now
  an omni whose body you are editing, one level deeper. Editing STAYS at that level until
  Shift-Tab climbs back out; consecutive same-level entries cost a Shift-Tab each. The rule is the
  same at every depth, the document root included. The one exception - a committed REFERENCE holds
  no children, so its Enter opens the next SIBLING hole.
- >
  **THE HEAD-OF-ROW EXCEPTION.** Enter with the caret at the very START of a committed value cell
  does not descend: the row is pushed DOWN and a fresh SIBLING entry hole opens BEFORE the entry,
  caret in it - the gesture every text editor gives Enter at a line's head. Only an ENTRY's row
  has a row above it; at the document root, and mid-text or at the end of the cell, the level rule
  stands.
- >
  **THE PROSE EXCEPTION.** Under the [chapter projection](::server:chapter-editor) Enter makes a
  SIBLING block, not a descent, and Tab is the only descent - prose is a stream of paragraphs, not
  a tree of typed values. In a LIST, Tab keeps the classic outliner move; inside a TABLE, Tab is a
  caret walk across the grid.
- >
  **THE REPRESENTATION RULE.** The concrete stores the REPRESENTATION and the renderer REPRODUCES
  it - never re-derives it from the decoded value. A quoted scalar keeps its quotes, a hex int its
  spelling, and a block scalar its AUTHORED header (`|`, `|-`, `|+`, `>`, ...) with its content
  lines exactly as they sit in the source. Editing keeps the representation too: committing a
  block cell re-emits the SAME header over the edited lines, and only a text that cannot live
  under a block header at all falls back to a quoted scalar. The self-value line's POSITION among
  the node's entries is representation too - saved at the position it was typed, never hoisted to
  the top.
- >
  **THE CONCRETE IS NOT A STATE.** The editor edits ANY yamlover-shaped node the same way - a file
  document, a directory, a directory-backed document. WHERE a newly typed child is STORED is
  decided server-side by the concrete-derivation rules (`concrete-rules.ts` + `concrete.ts`) -
  invisible to the editing states.
- >
  **Pseudo-states.** `nop`, `error_flash`, `focus_prev`, `focus_next` and `empty_cell_of_origin`
  stand for "consume the event and stay", "flash the red ring and stay", the two focus-walk
  actions whose next real state is the adjacent cell's focused state, and "return to the hole this
  cell grew from". Tab / Shift-Tab MOVE the focused entry and the FOCUS FOLLOWS it. The
  `open_brace` / `open_bracket` transitions apply IDENTICALLY at the document root, in entry holes
  and in value holes.
